many people ask me questions about stoic  philosophy and practice I've created  this series to provide short answers to  the most common recurring or confusing  questions about stoicism this question  what does a osis mean in a stoic context  is a very good one to ask whenever we're  encountering foreign language terms  being used quite frequently then we want  to know what they mean and I think that  wiki osis has been brought up an awful  lot in modern stoic context it is an  important term in classical stoic  ethical theory although it doesn't show  up that much in some of the texts that  we do have so it's well worth clarifying  what the term actually means and what  its significance is within a stoic  framework  Boyka iosys lexically speaking is a noun  that signifies a kind of action so it's  a noun that signifies a verb which is  also coming from another noun and that  is Waco's in classical Greek Waco's  means house but it doesn't just mean the  building that you might build and then  live in it means the entire extended  family and household you could say all  the people that are involved there so  that is the core idea that then is  getting transposed into a kind of action  what is that action then well it's been  translated in in various ways one is  affiliation or appropriation is another  endearment  I think another term that we could use  is concern once we get to the the  specifically stoic meaning of this term  and all of that should be read into this  it's not an easy term to translate but  it's not an impossible term to translate  either and each of those translations  that I've given  you can work in some situations what  then does this term mean within the  context of stoicism so the Stoics  treated this in a rather complex way and  it's important to maintain a distinction  that I'm now going to introduce you to  that's a distinction between a OSIS as  possessed by pretty much every animal  and then the okay osis that is  particular to human beings as rational  and thereby social animals different  from the other animals within the whole  kingdom of animal life so the Stoics say  that every animal has wakie osis in  relation to itself now what does this  mean it means that they want to preserve  their own life and find the things that  conduce to living well in their  particular way that conduced to living  and and continuing to augment that life  so that's part of it but it also  consists in a kind of self perception a  conception and and grasp of one's own  body in relation to other things and the  Stoics think that every single animal  has this and little babies for human  beings also have this egg that's the  first type and it gets a bit different  when we start talking about human beings  and there's a bit of analogy here other  animals at least some of them do take  care of their their young and sometimes  recognize others of their own kind in  ways that show concern as well and that  would be ok osis it's not Oh a OSIS of  their own bodies but that's ok OSIS in  relation to an other and for human  beings this becomes particularly  important it lies at the very basis of  really all of the relations of affection  or concern or even Duty that we have  towards  other people that we as rational beings  can recognize as part of our nature this  is what allows us the Sochi ability that  ultimately leads to stoic  cosmopolitanism so the idea is that not  only do we have you know perception of  ourselves and a kind of you know  ingrained desire to to remain living to  do it augmenting come to feel that and  even think that in relation to other  beings we can see them if we want to use  the metaphor of the eCos as part of our  own household in stoic ethics this is  going to play a very important role in  some of the authors who discuss it  explicitly like for example Cicero or  Heracles there will be this metaphor of  concentric circles each of them getting  larger and larger starting from the  individual person him or herself and  then you know another very small circle  friends and family and then extending  all the way out to encompass all human  beings or perhaps we might even say all  rational creatures within existence and  the idea that often gets talked about in  terms of these circles is that wake a  OSIS can extend from from the very small  circle where it's very easy to feel it  outward to these other circles this is  something that we can do by nature but  it's also something that we have to  cultivate to a certain degree and it  takes a bit of work so here at Cleese  we'll talk about making these circles  smaller that is a great way of  representing bringing everybody under  one great household and this would make  a lot of sense for for ancient thinkers  because another way of thinking about  the world or at least a civilization was  as a household and  we gonna make a an economy we often  translate that as but you could think of  it as the Commonwealth itself so this is  playing a really major role within stoic  ethics and it raises all sorts of  interesting questions of is this really  selfish or egoist or is it also  altruistic other oriented and the answer  is well it's actually both you it you  end up identifying yourself with other  people who then you come to care about  as if they were in fact yourself so  that's a really key conception to this  this whole idea of wonky osis this is  iosys in the second sense not just in  the sense of being concerned with  ourself and having self perception where  would you go if you wanted to learn more  about this particular conception of way  KO --ss well it does get mentioned a  little bit although not specifically as  such but in terms of other cognate saw  their words that are closely connected  with it in Diogenes Lyra tastes life of  Zeno it's an in book seven chapter one  of his lives of the philosopher so  that's one place to check out  Cicero also discusses this at  considerable length and in good detail 